WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.050
Hello, this is just a quick video before you begin

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.300
on what you're about to do and how the game works.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.850
So the board's eight by eight, and you and the computer

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.500
each have the same nine pieces, from one square up to four.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.600
Every new piece you place has to touch one of your own pieces

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.700
corner to corner, but can never touch along the side.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.500
Touching the computer's pieces, however, is fine.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.600
If you can't place anything, you're out and the computer carries on.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.200
When nobody can move, the game ends,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.900
and every square you didn't place counts against you.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.900
Each puzzle starts with one piece already down for each side.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.550
Sometimes you're Blue, sometimes Yellow.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.000
With perfect play you win by exactly one, and that's the best you can do.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.000
Every puzzle starts you on twenty points.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.900
Win by one, you keep the twenty. Draw and you get nineteen.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.600
Say you lose by three, you get sixteen.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.900
Some puzzles are stakes times two,

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.400
where every point you fall short costs you two,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.900
which you'll see before you start the puzzle.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.400
If you're stuck, you can buy a hint:

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.900
which piece, where to place it, or just the whole move if you get stuck.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.600
However, the catch: it will cost you points.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.700
The hints' prices are randomised each move,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.200
so it's up to you to decide if you think they're worth it.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.000
There are nine puzzles and you play each one once,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.600
and after each puzzle you see how you stack up against everyone else.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.600
Now you'll have a practice game and then a quick quiz on the rules,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.600
and then let the games begin!
